Q: Eclipse: How to load a huge class/interface (including 40000+ static fields) in a background thread A: <p>It's possible the classloader is blocking the UI thread trying to perform another classloading operation. Perhaps try loading the large class with another classloader to avoid blocking</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>URL[] urls = new URL[] { new File(&quot;c:/path/to/myjar.jar&quot;).toURI().toURL() };
ClassLoader classloader = new URLClassLoader(urls);
Class&lt;?&gt; intfType = classLoader.loadClass(&quot;My_Super_Big_Interface&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Note 1: This will have issues where a common class (eg model class used as a method argument) loaded by one classloader is not equal to the same class loaded by the other classloader.</p>
<p>Note 2: If you put the large class in it's own separate jar you could then remove that jar from the main classpath so it's impossible to load via the main classloader. In this case you could set the main classloader as a parent to the child classloader. This would allow you to pass model objects loaded by main classloader to the interface loaded by the child classloader without getting ClassCastException.</p>

Q: Dioxus futures confusion: Futures not being run A: <p>There's a few things I think are contributing to this.</p>
<p>Firstly, your SQL query looks malformed. I would expect it to contain a <code>SELECT</code> clause.</p>
<p><code>SELECT user_id FROM users WHERE user_id =$1</code></p>
<p>Whatever database you're using is probably failing to read your query and throwing back an error, which isn't being handled correctly and causes the thread to panic. Dioxus' terminal view is not like the one that gets displayed when running <code>cargo run</code>, which is why you're not getting any panic message.</p>
<p>Therefore, I would avoid using <code>panic!()</code> and suggest using Dioxus' in-built <code>tracing::debug!()</code> macro instead to feed information back to your terminal. Make sure to import <code>dioxus::logger</code> to get access to this.</p>
<p>I would also recommend short-handing your match statements for proper error propagation, as Dioxus' <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> type allows you to return any Errors that implement <code>std::Error</code>, which I believe the sqlx create does, to promote better troubleshooting.</p>
<pre><code>#[server]
// Explicitly stated the error return type (ServerFnError) - I believe Dioxus does this implicitly but, for the sake of clarity, have included it.
pub async fn get_name(user_id: i32) -&gt; Result&lt;String, ServerFnError&gt; {
    use sqlx::FromRow;

    // I changed your Query to include the SELECT clause and used the ? operator.
    let user_query_result = DATABASE
        .fetch_one(sqlx::query(&quot;SELECT name FROM users WHERE user_id = $1&quot;).bind(user_id))
        .await?;

    // Used the ? operator to return Result&lt;T, ServerFnError&gt;
    let sql_user_name = SqlUser::from_row(&amp;user_row)?;

    Ok(sql_user_name.get_name().to_string())
}
</code></pre>
<p>In your <code>Meal()</code> function, you're also not subscribing your view to <code>cook</code>'s the inner value - which can be achieved by using the <code>.read()</code> method on a signal. This will now update whenever the value changes.</p>
<p>I also think your <code>use_future()</code> is a bit cumbersome and could be simplified.</p>
<pre><code>#[component]
pub fn Meals() -&gt; Element {
    // Initialize an empty String.
    let mut name = use_signal(|| String::new());
    // Use server_resource() to fetch data from the back end and set the name value.
    let fetch = use_resource(move || async move {
        match get_name(2).await {
            // Set's the name signal
            Ok(v) =&gt; name.set(v),
            // Returns an error to your terminal
            Err(e) =&gt; tracing::debug!(&quot;{e:?}&quot;),
        };
    });

    // Subscribe to the signal using .read()
    rsx! {
        span { &quot;{name.read()}&quot; }
    }
} 
</code></pre>
<p>The end result should either set the value or return an error for you to debug.</p>
